Wow... that's some *nice* AC. Those stats are plenty good for
ascension. And you have *the entire castle wand* to spare?

Here's what you have left to gather:

0. A magic marker.
A single magic marker can map all of Gehennom with scrolls of magic
mapping (the cheapest scroll to write!). That alone is good reason for
the investment. You can *never* have too many of these. Writing a
cursed scroll of genocide for tengus or nurses in Sokoban is awesome.
Writing a scroll of taming to bring purple worms or minotaurs to you
aide is also awesome.
1. some cloak that provides MC3.
A dwarvish cloak's MC2 will save you from a footrice around 68% of the
time. An MC3 cloak will save you 98% of the time. Need I say more?
Either wish for a better cloak or enchant an arbitrary MC3 cloak, such
as an elven cloak, robe, oilskin cloak, or cloak of protection; the
latter will only decrease your AC by a single point, so there's little
disadvantage in switching. When you're in close quarters with
cockatrices and green slimes, trust me, you will be *glad* you've got
maximum magic cancellation to ward off their attacks. Displacement is
also an option, but won't matter as much since your AC is so good.
2. A good light source.
Your jumping boots are a lifesaver, but they are useless if you can't
see where to jump. Gehennom is dark. Light it up. If you can cast
"light" effectively, it's worth it. Otherwise have several oil lamps
at the ready. If you meet an aleax or angel with Sunsword, and kill
him while he's wielding it, it serves as a handy permanent light
source!
3. A healthy dose of genocide.
Get rid of L and h. Trust me, mind flayers are horrible creatures. If
you forget the scroll of charging after you run out of death charges,
or forget gold detection before the plane of air, you will rue the day
you gave that poor wittul starving master mind flayer a piece of your
brain. Don't get rid of disenchanters, as they are largely harmless if
you have MC3 (see above), and any weak monster next to you in a
summoning swarm is one less Jabberwock/pit fiend/mind flayer to
contend with.
4. Grease.
Grease your helmet if you keep flayers around. Grease your cloak if
you keep sea monsters around and keep it greased. You might choose an
oilskin cloak for this reason alone. It comes in handy at unexpected
moments, even when you're far from moats. And you may think this is
silly, but *grease your bag of holding*.  Though your chance of
diluting key resources during a swim is low due to your high luck,
it's nonzero, and keeping your bag greased will bring the chance to
zero. If you don't have a can of grease, it's easy to polypile for
one.
5. Good stuff *in open inventory*.
That means keeping wands of death, teleportation, and cold out at all
times. Cold will save your life from a giant eel, or from a mishap
with lava. Teleportation will buy you precious time to escape from
nasties, and can be broken in a grave emergency. Cancellation can save
your life if your bag of holding gets cursed, but it's best to avoid
this by keeping it blessed at all times.
6. Plenty of escape and healing resources.
Make sure you have five potions of full healing and four charged wands
of teleportation before waking Rodney. And identify your teleport
wands, because you don't want to zap an empty wand at an approaching
nasty. Having two wands of death, *also* identified, is good. And
above all, have blessed *and* cursed scrolls of blank paper at the
ready.
You should have a ton of scrolls of teleportation. Curse them to make
access to the Vibrating square and Moloch's sanctum easier; a
levelport into the sanctum can make grabbing the amulet much easier.
7. Some form of mapping or clairvoyance.
Gehennom's endless hedge-mazes are deadly boring. You could use a
magic marker to write scrolls of gold detection. If you're miserly,
buy clairvoyance from the Valley priest by giving him an amount of
gold between 200 and 400 times your current level (it also must be at
least half of your open inventory).
8. DON'T GET BURDENED.
Keep a stash close to the Valley, or possibly in Vlad's tower, for
ease of level-teleport. The moment you fall down the stairs wielding a
cockatrice corpse, you'll learn this lesson the hard way. Learn it the
easy way instead.
9. Take your time.
Don't rush to wake up that wizard. Have all the candles in the
candelabrum, and all the resources for an ascension in your BoH,
*before* you wake him up. All hell will break loose once you do. He
will wear you down if you give him the chance to do so.

Your next stop should be Gehennom since your character is plenty buff
enough. Demons drop wands of teleportation frequently, and there are
several no-teleport levels on which to collect those precious wands.
There's also scrolls of teleportation/create monster and potions of
full healing aplenty. Good luck and happy hacking!


